.TH CYAML_DELETE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cyaml_delete \- remove and free the DOM node at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cyaml.h>

ccol_retval_t cyaml_delete(cyaml root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The macro navigates to the parent of the node that the path addresses.
It removes the child and frees the whole subtree of that child
recursively.  The path uses the same dot-separated syntax as cyaml_get
and cyaml_set.

For a dictionary parent, the leaf component is the key to remove.  For
a list parent, the leaf component must be a '#N' token.  For example,
"#0" removes the first element.

The macro splits the path at the LAST dot that carries no escape.  The
path "a.b.c" therefore targets the key "c" inside the node at "a.b".
When the path holds no dot, root is the parent.

The escape sequences '\e.' and '\e\e' give a literal dot or a literal
backslash in a key name.  This matches cyaml_get and cyaml_set.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t.  The macro returns ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args comes back in six cases.  They are a NULL root, a
NULL path, and an empty path.  A component that is not valid for the
node that it addresses is the fourth case.  Bad '#N' index syntax is
the fifth, and an empty component is the sixth.  A
component that is not '#N' on a list is one invalid case.  Any
component that the macro finds under a scalar is another.  An empty
component is nothing between two dots, a leading dot, or a trailing
dot.  Any position in the path can give this code, not only the leaf.
ccol_key_not_found comes back when a component with correct syntax is
absent.  Such a component is a dictionary key or a '#N' index.  Any
position can give this code.  A list index with correct syntax that is
out of range gives it too.
ccol_not_enough_memory comes back on an internal allocation failure.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cyaml_delete(doc, "server.debug");
cyaml_delete(doc, "hosts.#0");
cyaml_delete(doc, "users.alice.address");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cyaml_get (3),
.BR cyaml_set (3),
.BR cyaml_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cyaml_list_remove (3)
